      "display_text_sha256": "e7eb3101ac99b43d1173dfe7bc378a8650d6d905fd9ee3abd14195dcc9bfd001",
      "speech_script": "Carrier mismatch is a receiver alignment problem, not automatically a failed transmitter. With equal carrier frequencies, a relative phase error phi scales the ideal recovered message by cos phi after gain correction. A 60 degree error leaves half amplitude; a 90 degree error suppresses the ideal message-band component. A finite filter can leave residual high-frequency ripple, so the observed trace need not be perfectly zero. A frequency mismatch adds a time-varying factor and can create a beating or distorted recovered waveform. Inspect the transmitter first, then compare the local oscillator frequency and phase with the carrier before changing unrelated blocks.",
